---
title: 'Shadow DOM'
description: '$framework Charts can render a chart in a shadow root of its own, so the page CSS cannot restyle it.'
---

A chart renders into the page's DOM, so a page rule that outranks the chart's own styles, such as one scoped by an ID, can restyle its controls. Set `enableShadowDom` to render the chart in a shadow root of its own, which page CSS cannot reach.

## Isolating a Chart from Page Styles

{% chartExampleRunner title="Page Styles" name="page-styles" type="generated" /%}

```js format="snippet"
{
    enableShadowDom: true,
}
```

In this example:

- The page has a `button:not(#toolbar button)` rule. The ID gives it enough weight to outrank the chart's styles, so it restyles the legend items.
- Select Shadow Root to set `enableShadowDom: true`, and the legend items go back to the chart's own styling.

Page CSS that targets chart elements, such as the `.ag-charts-tooltip` rules in [Tooltips](./tooltips/), does not reach a chart in a shadow root. Theme options and CSS variables still apply. Style and size the container itself, rather than selecting it through the chart's elements with a selector such as `:has(> .ag-charts-wrapper)`.

## Automatic Detection

When `enableShadowDom` is not set, the chart uses a shadow root only on known pages whose CSS breaks its controls, such as the claude.ai visualiser. Set `enableShadowDom: false` to keep the chart in the page's DOM on those pages.

